{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794590222510,"sessionID":"ses_e2a17c621ffemKP8pr6C6CtHPV","part":{"partID":"prt_1d5e9a2d6001fMH7XvnkrdEl8D","sessionID":"ses_e2a17c621ffemKP8pr6C6CtHPV","messageID":"msg_1d5e953d2001R0L7FB5rtMH09T","type":"tool","id":"call_db59a7820e8d467a89274660","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"for f in command-attach-guards.vitest.ts command-offer.vitest.ts command-transcript-score.vitest.ts command-bridge-resolution-guard.vitest.ts; do echo \"== $f\"; sed -n '1,12p' \".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/$f\"; done","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research"},"output":"== command-attach-guards.vitest.ts\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Command attach guards\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n// A hub route names the command that acts on the mode it picked, so it has to\n// honour the same no-edit wording the scorer honours. A reader who rules out a\n// change must not be handed a command that changes files.\n\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { join } from 'node:path';\n\nimport { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';\n== command-offer.vitest.ts\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Command offer\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n// The head is the only advisor text that names both the offered command and the\n// policy, so these cases pin each shape a live head takes, a command with a\n// policy, a command under an ambiguous head without one, and a head that names\n// no command at all. The replay case proves a checkout without the prompt hook\n// reports the miss, because a backfill off such a checkout has to stay readable\n// instead of throwing away the lines around it. The delivery cases pin the two\n// real answers a Claude transcript can give and the third answer a runtime with\n// no confirmed delivery point must give, so a later summary never reads a\n== command-transcript-score.vitest.ts\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Command Transcript Score\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n// Pins the facts one captured session is scored on, one behaviour per case, against\n// sessions built in the record shape the session readers hand over. Each case is a\n// turn a model could plausibly have taken: a reply that names no command, a document\n// read before and after the first write, a write under each policy, a mode the run\n// gave itself, a loop that ran its own steps before claiming its state and a\n// completion that worked ahead of the packet. The exit cases run the front door,\n// because a reader failure has to reach a caller as the reader's own code rather\n// than as a partial score, and the summary cases run it over a run log, because\n== command-bridge-resolution-guard.vitest.ts\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Command-Bridge → Live-Command Resolution Guard (023 WU5)\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// Every slash-command id cited by an advisor command bridge must resolve to a\n// real command file under `.skilled/commands/`. A dead id (e.g. a retired\n// `/deep:start-*-loop`) means a user typing the real command routes only by\n// natural-language luck, and a bridge that points at nothing silently rots.\n// Bridge-resolution checks cover the hand-authored surfaces (BASE_ALIAS_GROUPS\n// in aliases.ts and the inline COMMAND_BRIDGES in projection.ts). The dead-id\n// sweep additionally scans the emitted alias groups, the explicit-lane phrase\n// boosts, and the Python scorer: the projection hash proves those match the\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"== command-attach-guards.vitest.ts\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Command attach guards\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n// A hub route names the command that acts on the mode it picked, so it has to\n// honour the same no-edit wording the scorer honours. A reader who rules out a\n// change must not be handed a command that changes files.\n\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { join } from 'node:path';\n\nimport { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';\n== command-offer.vitest.ts\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Command offer\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n// The head is the only advisor text that names both the offered command and the\n// policy, so these cases pin each shape a live head takes, a command with a\n// policy, a command under an ambiguous head without one, and a head that names\n// no command at all. The replay case proves a checkout without the prompt hook\n// reports the miss, because a backfill off such a checkout has to stay readable\n// instead of throwing away the lines around it. The delivery cases pin the two\n// real answers a Claude transcript can give and the third answer a runtime with\n// no confirmed delivery point must give, so a later summary never reads a\n== command-transcript-score.vitest.ts\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Command Transcript Score\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n// Pins the facts one captured session is scored on, one behaviour per case, against\n// sessions built in the record shape the session readers hand over. Each case is a\n// turn a model could plausibly have taken: a reply that names no command, a document\n// read before and after the first write, a write under each policy, a mode the run\n// gave itself, a loop that ran its own steps before claiming its state and a\n// completion that worked ahead of the packet. The exit cases run the front door,\n// because a reader failure has to reach a caller as the reader's own code rather\n// than as a partial score, and the summary cases run it over a run log, because\n== command-bridge-resolution-guard.vitest.ts\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Command-Bridge → Live-Command Resolution Guard (023 WU5)\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// Every slash-command id cited by an advisor command bridge must resolve to a\n// real command file under `.skilled/commands/`. A dead id (e.g. a retired\n// `/deep:start-*-loop`) means a user typing the real command routes only by\n// natural-language luck, and a bridge that points at nothing silently rots.\n// Bridge-resolution checks cover the hand-authored surfaces (BASE_ALIAS_GROUPS\n// in aliases.ts and the inline COMMAND_BRIDGES in projection.ts). The dead-id\n// sweep additionally scans the emitted alias groups, the explicit-lane phrase\n// boosts, and the Python scorer: the projection hash proves those match the\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"for f in command-attach-guards.vitest.ts command-offer.vitest.ts command-transcript-score.vitest.ts command-bridge-resolution-guard.vitest.ts; do echo \\\"== $f\\\"; sed -n '1,12p' \\\".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/$f\\\"; done\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research\"}"},"time":{"start":1794590221014,"end":1794590222510}}}}
